import json, re, collections
B = [
"A couple sells the house they bought for one hundred forty thousand dollars. The buyer pays nine hundred thousand. It is easy to assume that after sixty-five, that profit is simply tax free. It is not. The I R S allows a big exclusion, but part of this couple's profit is still taxed, and it can also raise their Medicare bill two years later. Here are the exact numbers, from the official rules.",
"Meet Ray and Donna, both seventy-two, married, filing jointly. Long ago they paid one hundred forty thousand for their home, and they spent another sixty thousand on a new roof and a kitchen. Now they plan to sell for nine hundred thousand and move closer to their daughter. Their other income, from pensions and retirement withdrawals, is about ninety thousand dollars a year.",
"Every rule here comes from official sources: I R S Publication five twenty-three, the I R S tax topic pages, the Social Security Administration's rules on Medicare premiums, and the Medicare fact sheets. We will cover five things: who qualifies, how the gain is counted and taxed, what it does to Medicare and Social Security, the cases that change the amount, and what happens to your family.",
"Number one is who qualifies, and the I R S lets you exclude up to two hundred fifty thousand dollars of profit when you sell your main home, or up to five hundred thousand dollars if you are married and file a joint return. To get it, you must pass two tests: you owned the home for at least twenty-four months, and you lived in it as your main home for at least twenty-four months, all within the five years before the sale.",
"Some details matter. The twenty-four months do not have to be in a row; they only have to add up to seven hundred thirty days. Only one spouse has to pass the ownership test, but both must pass the living-in-it test. You can use the exclusion once every two years, and only on your main home. And if you cannot care for yourself, time in a licensed nursing home can count as time living there, if the home was your main home for at least twelve months in the last five years.",
"Next is the math of the gain, and the I R S formula is simple. Take the selling price, and subtract your selling costs, like the agent's commission, advertising and legal fees. That gives you the amount realized. Then subtract your adjusted basis, which is what you invested in the home. What is left is your gain. If it is negative, you have a loss, and the I R S says you cannot deduct a loss on your home.",
"Your basis is more than the price you paid, because it includes closing costs like legal fees, recording fees, transfer taxes and owner's title insurance, plus improvements: a new roof, a kitchen remodel, an addition, a new heating system. It does not include repairs like painting or fixing a leak, or loan fees and points. Keep your receipts: the I R S says to keep records until three years after the due date of the return for the year you sell.",
"Now the math for Ray and Donna. Their basis is the one hundred forty thousand they paid plus sixty thousand in improvements, so two hundred thousand dollars. They sell at nine hundred thousand and pay fifty-four thousand in selling costs, so the amount realized is eight hundred forty-six thousand. Subtract the basis, and their gain is six hundred forty-six thousand dollars. Their exclusion is five hundred thousand, so one hundred forty-six thousand dollars is taxable.",
"Notice what the receipts did for Ray and Donna. Without them, the basis would be only one hundred forty thousand, and the taxable gain would be two hundred six thousand. That is sixty thousand dollars of extra profit on paper, just because the receipts for the roof and the kitchen were lost.",
"The federal tax on that gain depends on how long they owned the home. It was more than a year, so it is a long-term capital gain, taxed at zero, fifteen or twenty percent. For twenty twenty-six, a joint return pays zero percent up to ninety-eight thousand nine hundred dollars of taxable income, and fifteen percent up to six hundred thirteen thousand seven hundred. So Ray and Donna owe at most about twenty-one thousand nine hundred dollars, and less if part falls in the zero bracket.",
"Two more notes. A three point eight percent net investment income tax applies only above two hundred fifty thousand dollars of income on a joint return, or two hundred thousand if single, and the excluded profit does not count. Ray and Donna stay under it. State taxes are separate from these rules. And if you claim depreciation on a rental or business part of the home after May of nineteen ninety-seven, that gain cannot be excluded.",
"Then the paperwork. If you receive Form ten ninety-nine S, you must report the sale on Form eighty-nine forty-nine, even if all of the gain is excluded. And if you owe tax, you may also need to raise your withholding or make estimated payments, so the bill does not surprise you next spring.",
"Number three is the ripples, and this is where many people get surprised. The taxable gain lands on the tax return, and Ray and Donna's income for that year jumps from ninety thousand to two hundred thirty-six thousand dollars. Medicare generally sets your Part B premium using the income on your tax return from two years earlier. The Social Security Administration's manual gives this example: twenty twenty-six premiums are generally based on twenty twenty-four returns.",
"Using the twenty twenty-six brackets, a joint return above two hundred eighteen thousand dollars pays more. Between two hundred eighteen and two hundred seventy-four thousand, each person pays eighty-one dollars and twenty cents more per month, for a total of two hundred eighty-four dollars and ten cents. For Ray and Donna, that is one thousand nine hundred forty-eight dollars and eighty cents extra for the year. Future brackets will differ, so this is only an illustration.",
"The same gain can also touch your Social Security. The I R S says your benefits become taxable when half of your benefits plus your other income is above a base amount: twenty-five thousand for a single filer, or thirty-two thousand on a joint return. Above thirty-four thousand, or forty-four thousand on a joint return, up to eighty-five percent of your benefits can be taxable. A big gain can push you higher in the year of the sale.",
"Number four is the exceptions that change your limit, and the first is a partial exclusion. If you miss the two-year tests, you can still get part of it when the main reason you sold was a work move, a health reason, or an unforeseeable event. Health includes moving for treatment, caring for a sick family member, or a doctor's advice. The formula: months lived there, divided by twenty-four, times two hundred fifty thousand. Twelve months gives a single owner one hundred twenty-five thousand.",
"Second, the surviving spouse. Suppose Ray passes away, and Donna decides to sell. If she sells within two years of his death, and is not remarried, she can still use the five hundred thousand dollar exclusion, as long as the other tests are met, and she can count the time Ray owned and lived in the home. After the two years, the limit falls back to the single amount, two hundred fifty thousand. So for a widow or widower, the date on the calendar matters.",
"There is another benefit for a survivor. For a jointly owned home, the half that belonged to the spouse who died gets a new basis equal to its value on that date. In the I R S example, a home with a basis of fifty thousand dollars is worth one hundred thousand, and the survivor's new basis becomes seventy-five thousand. In the nine community property states, the whole home usually gets the new basis.",
"Number five is your family, and the rules are very different depending on how the home passes on. If your children inherit it, their basis is its value when you die, so the profit built up before then is not taxed when they sell. But if you give them the home while you are alive, the I R S says that as a general rule they take your basis. In Ray and Donna's case, the gift basis would be two hundred thousand, not nine hundred thousand, and a child who never lived there may not get the exclusion.",
"What should you do before you sign? One: count your dates, including the two-year window if you are a widow or widower. Two: gather your closing papers and improvement receipts. Three: estimate your gain with the free worksheets in Publication five twenty-three. Four: check what a gain would do to your Medicare premiums and Social Security tax. Five: the sale date is usually the closing date, so it decides which tax year the gain lands in.",
"To sum up. One: owning and living in your home for twenty-four months within five years lets you exclude two hundred fifty thousand, or five hundred thousand jointly. Two: gain is price minus costs minus basis, and improvements raise basis. Three: a taxable gain can lift your Medicare costs and the tax on your Social Security. Four: partial and survivor rules change the limit. Five: an inherited home gets a new basis, while a gift usually keeps yours.",
"If this helped, send it to someone who is thinking about selling their home, and tell me in the comments: would you sell, or stay? One last thing: this video is general information, not tax advice. Rules, amounts and ages change and differ by location, so confirm with the I R S or a qualified tax professional before you act. This channel is not affiliated with the I R S, Medicare or Social Security.",
]
J = {str(i + 1): b for i, b in enumerate(B)}
json.dump({'B': J}, open('code/blk.json', 'w'), indent=0)
bad = 0
for i, b in enumerate(B, 1):
    flag = ''
    if len(b) > 500: flag += ' >500'; bad += 1
    if re.search(r'\d', b): flag += ' CIFRE'; bad += 1
    print(i, len(b), len(b.split()), flag)
print('TOT', sum(len(b) for b in B), sum(len(b.split()) for b in B), 'min a 145wpm ~', round(sum(len(b.split()) for b in B) / 145, 1))
W = [(i + 1, re.findall(r"[a-z']+", b.lower())) for i, b in enumerate(B)]
seen = collections.defaultdict(set)
for i, w in W:
    for k in range(len(w) - 4): seen[tuple(w[k:k + 5])].add(i)
for k, v in seen.items():
    if len(v) > 1: print('RIPETUTA', ' '.join(k), sorted(v))
